assertion  XSH.fpclassify.001
anchor     functions/fpclassify.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fpclassify.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fpclassify() of +0 and -0 is FP_ZERO; of 1, -1, 0.1, MIN, -MIN, MAX
           and -MAX (MIN, MAX the smallest normal and largest finite values of
           the type) it is FP_NORMAL, for arguments of each of the three floating
           types.
test       t/001-zero-normal.c

assertion  XSH.fpclassify.002
anchor     functions/fpclassify.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fpclassify.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fpclassify() of +Inf and -Inf is FP_INFINITE, for each of the three
           floating types.
test       t/002-infinite.c

assertion  XSH.fpclassify.003
anchor     functions/fpclassify.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fpclassify.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fpclassify() of a NaN and of its negation is FP_NAN, for each of the
           three floating types.
test       t/003-nan.c

assertion  XSH.fpclassify.004
anchor     functions/fpclassify.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fpclassify.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fpclassify() of half the smallest normal value of the type, and of its
           negation, is FP_SUBNORMAL when the type has subnormal values.
test       t/004-subnormal.c

assertion  XSH.fpclassify.005
anchor     functions/fpclassify.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fpclassify.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For float and double, fpclassify(MAX * 2), with MAX the largest finite
           value of the type, is FP_INFINITE: a product evaluated in a wider
           format is converted to the semantic type first.
test       t/005-semantic-type.c

assertion  XSH.fpclassify.006
anchor     basedefs/math.h.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fpclassify.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fpclassify.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       FP_INFINITE, FP_NAN, FP_NORMAL, FP_SUBNORMAL and FP_ZERO are integer
           constant expressions with distinct values (a switch statement uses
           them as case labels), and fpclassify() returns one of them for 1.0.
test       t/006-distinct-macros.c

assertion  XSH.fpclassify.007
anchor     basedefs/math.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       MATH_ERRNO is 1 and MATH_ERREXCEPT is 2 (integer constant
           expressions), math_errhandling is an int equal to 1, 2 or 3, and when
           its MATH_ERREXCEPT bit is set FE_DIVBYZERO, FE_INVALID and FE_OVERFLOW
           are defined.
test       t/007-math-errhandling.c

assertion  XSH.fpclassify.008
anchor     basedefs/math.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       HUGE_VAL, HUGE_VALF and HUGE_VALL are greater than zero and, where the
           IEC 60559 option is claimed, are +Inf; INFINITY is then +Inf and NAN
           (when defined) is a NaN.
note       The infinity part of this assertion is for systems that claim the IEC
           60559 option (Annex F of the C standard); positivity is required
           everywhere.
test       t/008-huge-val.c
